  "worked_solution": "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{8},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14128542/assets/5eb48411cf0c9efd97bc99a4c5f6edd064664c98eeef87ceda6d9909a5056eab.png)",
